   licensed under the MIT License: the eigendecomposition gradient in
   wavebit/_core/backend.py is a port of `_eig_bwd` from that project's
   src/fmmax/utils.py. The MIT License requires that its copyright notice and
   permission notice be retained; both are reproduced in full in the file
   LICENSE.fmmax.

   fmmax's maintained fork (https://github.com/invrs-io/fmmax) carries the same
   function in src/fmmax/eig.py and re-states its copyright as 'Copyright: 2025
   invrs.io LLC', under the same MIT terms. Which of the two the port was taken
   from is not recorded, so both notices are stated here. LICENSE.fmmax itself is
   kept as the verbatim upstream text and nothing else, so that licence scanners
   identify it as MIT -- a note appended there is 29% of MIT's short body and
   drops the file below the match threshold GitHub's `licensee` uses.

   ---
   The Li-rule Fourier factorization of rasters in wavebit/_core/factorization.py
   was rewritten on 2026-09-25 from L. Li's published factorization rules
   (J. Opt. Soc. Am. A 13, 1870 (1996); 14, 2758 (1997)): li_conv_matrices,
   li_conv_matrices_integrated and the helpers written with them, the bodies of
   fourier2d_piecewise_constant, fourier2d_pointwise and fourier2d_integrated,
   and the Li parts of to_conv_mat_raster_sampled and
   to_conv_mat_raster_integrated. The implementer of that rewrite did not
   consult RETICOLO's or meent's source or the bodies of the functions it
   replaced. compression_indices and compress_cell were then reimplemented by
   an implementer working from a written specification of what they return, in
   a copy of this repository without its history or the earlier bodies; the
   specification's author had seen the earlier functions and RETICOLO's ret2li.
   The other Li factorizations run on the rewrite as well: the Cartesian shape
   path (wavebit/_core/rectilinear.py) through fourier2d_integrated, and the
   tensor path's row transform (wavebit/_core/tensor_li.py, added 2026-09-24),
   which called the earlier helpers directly until it was moved onto the
   rewritten series. That move, and _row_dft, which restates the documented
   rule of _normalized_row_fft (a JAX workaround added beside _fourier2d_grid
   on 2026-09-24 and deleted with it), were written by an implementer working
   from written specifications whose authors had seen the earlier bodies. The
   rewritten code still calls helpers kept from before it, written in this
   repository in August 2026: _pixel_correction, _half_pixel_phase and
   _inverse_axes. It also calls _circulant_index, which came with the
   2026-08-03 single-source port of meent's dfs2d and was rewritten from its
   definition on 2026-09-26 by an implementer who had not read the earlier
   body. The commit messages of those dates, and of later changes, record how.

   The rewritten builders' default nesting ('fixed') corresponds to the
   default, parm.res1.li = 1, of 'RETICOLO', the grating software of J.P.
   Hugonin and P. Lalanne (Institut d'Optique/CNRS): J.P. Hugonin and P.
   Lalanne, "RETICOLO software for grating analysis", Institut d'Optique,
   Orsay, France (2005), arXiv:2101.00901. Their 'symmetric' nesting
   corresponds to RETICOLO's parm.res1.li = 0.

   Earlier versions of those functions remain in this repository's history, with
   the helpers deleted alongside them (_fourier2d_grid, _fourier1d_integrated,
   _wrap and _circulant_gather); that history includes meent's own, from 2022.
   The Fourier builders descend from meent's dfs2d and cfs2d, written in July
   2024 with reference to the MATLAB source of RETICOLO V9, following its
   ret2li (in retcouche.m), retf.m and rettoeplitz.m; compression_indices and
   compress_cell were split from meent's cell_compression (2022). meent's code
   names RETICOLO elsewhere in that history too: fft_piecewise_constant was
   marked "reference: reticolo" from 2023-02 to 2024-06, and a commented-out
   dfs2d_debug in meent/on_numpy/emsolver/fourier_analysis.py, added on
   2024-07-19, is described as "algorithm from reticolo", as it still is in
   meent's own repository (commit bae2802). Checked on 2026-09-25 against
   RETICOLO's Zenodo records and the packages themselves: retcouche.m, retf.m
   and rettoeplitz.m are byte-identical in V9
   (https://zenodo.org/records/4419063, 2021), in its revision V9a
   (https://zenodo.org/records/5905381, 2022) and in V10
   (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14631951, January 2025). The V9 and V9a
   records declare no licence; the V10 record states CC BY 4.0. No package
   carries a licence file or licence text in its source. Each package's
   documentation states "copyright (c) 2005 Institut d'Optique/CNRS" and
   requires that publications and reports cite the reference above; V10's also
   calls RETICOLO a freeware. The V10 record also names itself a new version
   of V9 (isNewVersionOf 10.5281/zenodo.4419063).

   Those earlier versions, and the meent code named above, are used under the
   Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC BY 4.0) that the
   V10 record states. They were modified: written in Python, first in meent
   (https://github.com/kc-ml2/meent) and then in this repository, with
   reference to RETICOLO's MATLAB source, and the earlier versions have since
   been replaced by the rewrite described above. Attribution:

       RETICOLO, by J.P. Hugonin and P. Lalanne
       copyright (c) 2005 Institut d'Optique/CNRS
       Material: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14631951
       Citation: arXiv:2101.00901
       Licence: CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

   Section 5 of that licence (Disclaimer of Warranties and Limitation of
   Liability) applies to that material. RETICOLO itself is not part of this
   software; the comparisons in tests/references/ call a separately installed
   copy, and one bridge script, tests/references/interface/reti_aniso.m, lays
   out its calls after RETICOLO's example exemple_V9_0D_anisotrope.m.
   tests/references/README.md records the check.
